Building jlib on Linux via Docker
=================================

The source is copied into the image and compiled during the image build, not
bind-mounted.  On macOS a bind mount crosses the VM's filesystem
virtualization layer, which makes C++ compilation dramatically slower.

    # build the toolchain image and compile jlib inside it
    docker build -t jlib-build .

    # drop into a shell on the built tree
    docker run --rm -ti jlib-build

Inside the container the build lives at /src/jlib/build:

    cd /src/jlib/build
    make check
    make -j"$(nproc)"

configure prints a feature summary saying which optional modules were enabled
and which were skipped for want of a dependency.  On Linux it should report
x11, glx and the joystick API as present; on macOS those are absent and the
GL/GLU/GLUT frameworks are used instead.

Dependencies install in their own layer, so editing source re-runs only the
autogen/configure/make steps.  A .dockerignore keeps the host's build trees
and generated autotools output out of the image so the container always
bootstraps for its own platform.

Note: the crypt_groth_test allocates heavily.  If it is killed with exit 137
the container hit its memory ceiling -- raise the Docker VM's RAM allocation.
